WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
Starch and cellulose are both chains of the same sugar, glucose.

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.560
So why can your own enzymes take starch apart, but not cellulose?

3
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:18.293
The first film showed food taken apart by adding water, one water per link.

4
00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:21.373
Here are the same gold sugar blocks.

5
00:00:22.573 --> 00:00:24.173
But their links are not all alike.

6
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:29.653
In starch, the glucose links let the chain coil, and some starch branches.

7
00:00:30.993 --> 00:00:35.273
It is the plant's store of sugar, and your enzymes can take it apart for energy.

8
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:40.133
A digestive enzyme is a protein folded into a pocket.

9
00:00:41.613 --> 00:00:43.013
Starch's links fit that pocket.

10
00:00:44.173 --> 00:00:48.533
The enzyme adds water at a link, and the chain breaks into smaller sugars.

11
00:00:49.973 --> 00:00:52.613
Other enzymes finish the job, down to glucose.

12
00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:57.897
In cellulose, the glucose blocks are linked the other way.

13
00:00:59.157 --> 00:01:01.617
The chains lie straight and stack side by side.

14
00:01:02.857 --> 00:01:06.257
Together they form strong fibers that stiffen plant cell walls.

15
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.050
Now the same enzyme tries cellulose.

16
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.950
Its pocket does not fit these links.

17
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.150
Your own enzymes cannot break them.

18
00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.190
So for you, cellulose is fiber.

19
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:23.323
Same sugar, different links, two shapes, two jobs.

20
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:27.443
One stores energy; the other stiffens plant walls.

21
00:01:28.623 --> 00:01:32.443
The blocks, the links between them and the shape they take decide the job.

22
00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:35.577
The enzyme follows the same rule.

23
00:01:36.757 --> 00:01:40.717
It is a protein, a chain of amino acids folded into a shape.

24
00:01:41.857 --> 00:01:46.157
That folded shape makes the pocket, and the pocket fits one kind of molecule.

25
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:49.747
Lipids follow the rule too.

26
00:01:51.007 --> 00:01:55.807
A phospholipid has a head that is attracted to water and two tails that avoid it.

27
00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.120
Picture many phospholipids dropped into water, all tumbling at once.

28
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.440
Each head likes water, and each pair of tails avoids it.

29
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:07.900
Which way will the heads point?

30
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:16.623
Out, toward the water, on both sides.

31
00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:19.303
The tails tuck into the middle.

32
00:02:20.423 --> 00:02:24.143
That double layer is the basic structure of a cell membrane.

33
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:28.207
Fats are lipids too, but a fat is not a chain.

34
00:02:29.527 --> 00:02:32.627
One glycerol holds three fatty acids by three links.

35
00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:36.647
Count from the free pieces, and three waters are missing.

36
00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:39.387
The water count still works.

37
00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:42.007
The chain picture does not.

38
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.340
Counting water only tells you how many links there are.

39
00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:48.800
It does not tell you the job.

40
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.860
The blocks, the links between them and the shape they take decide what each molecule

41
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:55.440
can do.
